{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1792653404.7000427, "id": "14b023096dc7", "tool_call_id": "call_00_gSiAB8YczYAgE4FcF8Zd2030", "content": "ERROR: command failed (exit 1)\nstdout:\ntarget: 4 armors x 40 steps, body at [3.  0.  0.5]\ncamera: 1280x1024 fx=800.0 fy=800.0 pos=[0.  0.  0.5] yaw=0.0 deg\n\nstderr:\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/scripts/visualize_target.py\", line 459, in <module>\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n                     ^^^^^^\n  File \"/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/scripts/visualize_target.py\", line 442, in main\n    run_self_test(scene, cam, cam_pos)\n  File \"/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/scripts/visualize_target.py\", line 345, in run_self_test\n    assert u_yaw < u_ref\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nAssertionError\n", "is_error": true, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1792653414.0942705, "id": "f634cd4d1db6", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_JSikrxG3EOoH8LME38uX5048", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'PY'\\np = \\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\"\\ns = open(p, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").read()\\nold = \\\"\\\"\\\"    # yawing left moves a target at world +Y further left in the image\\n    probe = np.array([[3.0, 0.3, 0.0]])\\n    R_yaw = camera_from_world(math.radians(30.0), 0.0)\\n    u_yaw = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_yaw))[0][0]\\n    u_ref = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_wc))[0][0]\\n    assert u_yaw < u_ref\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew = \\\"\\\"\\\"    # positive camera yaw = look left (CCW about world +Z): the optical axis\\n    # tips towards +Y, so a target on the left drifts right inside the image\\n    probe = np.array([[3.0, 0.3, 0.0]])\\n    R_yaw = camera_from_world(math.radians(30.0), 0.0)\\n    axis = R_yaw[2]\\n    assert axis[0] > 0.5 and axis[1] > 0.5\\n    u_yaw = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_yaw))[0][0]\\n    u_ref = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_wc))[0][0]\\n    assert u_yaw > u_ref\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nassert old in s\\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\\nopen(p, \\\"w\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").write(s)\\nprint(\\\"patched\\\")\\nPY\\n.venv/bin/python scripts/visualize_target.py --test\"}"}], "reasoning": "My yaw sign assumption: with camera yaw +30°, does a target at +Y move left or right in the image?\n\nR_wc(yaw) = base @ Rz(-yaw). A target at world +Y: cam coords = base @ Rz(-yaw) @ (0,0.3,0).\n\nRz(-yaw) with yaw=30: [[c, s,0],[-s,c,0],[0,0,1]] applied to (0,0.3,0) → (s*0.3, c*0.3, 0) = (0.15, 0.26, 0).\nbase → cam = (-y, -z, x) = (-0.26, 0, 0.15)... wait base maps (a,b,c) → (0*a -1*b + 0*c, 0*a+0*b-1*c, 1*a+0*b+0*c) = (-b, -c, a) = (-0.26, 0, 0.15).\n\nHmm, that gives camera x = -0.26 → u = cx + 800*(-0.26)/z where z is the total depth (rel also includes (3,0,0) component). Let me compute properly with the full probe point (3, 0.3, 0) minus cam_pos (0,0,0.5) = (3, 0.3, -0.5).\n\nWait, cam_pos z=0.5, probe z=0 → rel z = -0.5.\n\nFor yaw=0: R_wc @ rel = (-0.3, 0.5, 3) → u = cx + 800*(-0.3)/3 = cx - 80 (left ✓), v = cy - 800*0.5/3 = cy - 133 (above center, since probe is 0.5 below camera? Hmm: rel z (world) = -0.5, camera Y = -world Z = -(-0.5) = +0.5 → down 133 px. Correct, probe is below camera.)\n\nFor yaw=+30°: Rz(-30) @ rel = (c*3 + s*0.3, -s*3 + c*0.3, -0.5) = (0.866*3 + 0.5*0.3, -0.5*3 + 0.866*0.3, -0.5) = (2.748, -1.240, -0.5).\nbase → cam = (-b, -c, a) = (1.240, 0.5, 2.748).\nu = cx + 800*1.240/2.748 = cx + 361 → to the RIGHT.\n\nHmm — so positive cam_yaw turns the camera *right*, i.e., it yaws clockwise (toward +... ). In world terms with Y left, \"turning right\" means rotating toward -Y, which is yaw negative under CCW convention. My formula gives: cam yaw positive → camera looks toward +Y (left) ... wait let me check: with yaw=+30°, where does the optical axis point? Camera's forward direction in world = R_wc^T @ [0,0,1] = third column of R_wc^T = third row of R_wc. R_wc = base @ Rz(-yaw). Let me compute forward world vector: cam forward e_z in world = R_wc^T e_z. \n\nR_wc = base @ Rz(-yaw). base rows: [0,-1,0],[0,0,-1],[1,0,0]. R_wc = base @ Rz(-30):\nRow0 of base = (0,-1,0) → times Rz(-30) → row = -(row1 of Rz(-30)) = -(-s, c, 0) = (s, -c, 0) = (0.5, -0.866, 0).\nRow2 of base = (1,0,0) → (c, s, 0) = (0.866, 0.5, 0).\nSo R_wc = [[0.5,-0.866,0],[0,0,-1],[0.866,0.5,0]] (row1 = (0,0,-1)).\n\nForward in world = third row of R_wc = (0.866, 0.5, 0) → points +X and +Y, i.e. to the LEFT. So with cam_yaw = +30°, the camera looks left by 30°. Then a target at world +Y (left) should move to the RIGHT side of the image... no wait: if the camera turns left, objects shift right in the image. Yes — camera looks left, so the target appears more to the right. That's what we computed (u increased).\n\nSo my assertion was wrong: `u_yaw < u_ref` should be `u_yaw > u_ref` given cam_yaw>0 = look left.\n\nHmm, but is \"positive yaw = look left\" the desired convention? Original code used `Rz(-cam_yaw)` too, so its convention is the same as mine: positive = look left (CCW about +Z, matching world Y-left CCW convention where +yaw is CCW viewed from above = turning left). Consistent with the body's yaw in BodyMotion (yaw_rate positive = CCW). Good — keep it and fix the test to assert `u_yaw > u_ref`, and add a comment.\n\nLet me also double check the image-shift direction logic: camera yaw +30 = look left by 30°; the target at world +Y=0.3 which is left of the initial axis: after looking left, target moves toward image center/right → u increases ✓.\n\nFix the test lines.\n\nAlso verify the \"world left → camera -X\" check passed (it did, since the failure was later).\n\nPatch: replace\n```\n    # yawing left moves a target at world +Y further left in the image\n    probe = np.array([[3.0, 0.3, 0.0]])\n    R_yaw = camera_from_world(math.radians(30.0), 0.0)\n    u_yaw = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_yaw))[0][0]\n    u_ref = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_wc))[0][0]\n    assert u_yaw < u_ref\n```\nwith a version asserting u_yaw > u_ref and checking the optical axis direction.\n\nNew block:\n```python\n    # positive camera yaw = look left (CCW about world +Z): the optical axis\n    # tips towards +Y and a target on the left drifts right in the image\n    probe = np.array([[3.0, 0.3, 0.0]])\n    R_yaw = camera_from_world(math.radians(30.0), 0.0)\n    axis = R_yaw[2]\n    assert axis[0] > 0 and axis[1] > 0\n    u_yaw = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_yaw))[0][0]\n    u_ref = cam.project(world_to_camera(probe, cam_pos, R_wc))[0][0]\n    assert u_yaw > u_ref\n```\nNote: forward world = third row of R_wc = R_wc[2] ✓.\n\nCareful with apostrophes: \"camera yaw\" no apostrophes. Good.\n\nUse python patch heredoc, no apostrophes."}
