{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1792654110.7847729, "id": "b58e37cd484e", "tool_call_id": "call_00_U1moguetp6qgGxhc33ug3634", "content": "OK: command succeeded\nstdout:\ndiff --git a/diff_env/target.py b/diff_env/target.py\nindex 864de21..1f45e33 100644\n--- a/diff_env/target.py\n+++ b/diff_env/target.py\n@@ -60,13 +60,13 @@ class BodyMotion(nn.Module):\n                 2 * math.pi * yaw_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t\n             )\n \n-        pitch = pitch_0.unsqueeze(1)\n+        pitch = pitch_0.unsqueeze(1).expand(-1, sln)\n         if pitch_amp.abs().sum() > 0:\n             pitch = pitch + pitch_amp.unsqueeze(1) * torch.sin(\n                 2 * math.pi * pitch_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t\n             )\n \n-        roll = roll_0.unsqueeze(1)\n+        roll = roll_0.unsqueeze(1).expand(-1, sln)\n         if roll_amp.abs().sum() > 0:\n             roll = roll + roll_amp.unsqueeze(1) * torch.sin(\n                 2 * math.pi * roll_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t\n@@ -87,8 +87,9 @@ class ArmorLayout(nn.Module):\n         self,\n         n_armors: int,\n         radius: torch.Tensor,\n-        radius_offsets: torch.Tensor = None,\n-        height_offsets: torch.Tensor = None,\n+        radius_offsets: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n+        height_offsets: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n+        armor_pitch: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n     ):\n         device = radius.device\n         bsz = radius.shape[0]\n@@ -101,6 +102,14 @@ class ArmorLayout(nn.Module):\n             height_offsets = torch.zeros(\n                 bsz, n_armors, device=device, dtype=radius.dtype\n             )\n+        if armor_pitch is None:\n+            armor_pitch = torch.full(\n+                (bsz, n_armors), math.radians(15.0), device=device, dtype=radius.dtype\n+            )\n+        elif not isinstance(armor_pitch, torch.Tensor):\n+            armor_pitch = torch.full(\n+                (bsz, n_armors), float(armor_pitch), device=device, dtype=radius.dtype\n+            )\n \n         theta = (\n             torch.arange(n_armors, device=device, dtype=radius.dtype)\n@@ -109,14 +118,17 @@ class ArmorLayout(nn.Module):\n             / n_armors\n         )\n \n-        local_x = radius.unsqueeze(-1) * torch.cos(theta)\n-        local_y = radius.unsqueeze(-1) * torch.sin(theta)\n+        r_eff = radius.unsqueeze(-1) + radius_offsets\n+        local_x = r_eff * torch.cos(theta)\n+        local_y = r_eff * torch.sin(theta)\n         local_z = height_offsets\n         local_positions = torch.stack([local_x, local_y, local_z], dim=-1)\n \n-        yaws = theta.unsqueeze(0)\n-        zeros = torch.zeros_like(yaws)\n-        local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, zeros)\n+        yaws = theta.unsqueeze(0).expand(bsz, -1)\n+        # upright plate: width (+X) tangential, height (+Y) up, normal (+Z)\n+        # radially outward and lifted by armor_pitch\n+        roll = torch.full_like(yaws, math.pi / 2)\n+        local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws + math.pi / 2, -armor_pitch, roll)\n \n         w2 = self.armor_width / 2\n         h2 = self.armor_height / 2\n@@ -135,13 +147,13 @@ class ArmorLayout(nn.Module):\n \n \n class TargetMotion(nn.Module):\n-    def __init__(self, armor_width: float = 0.02, armor_height: float = 0.06):\n+    def __init__(self, armor_width: float = 0.12, armor_height: float = 0.06):\n         super().__init__()\n         self.body_motion = BodyMotion()\n         self.armor_layout = ArmorLayout(armor_width, armor_height)\n \n     def forward(self, t, mode=\"static\", **params):\n-        bsz, sln = t.shape\n+        bsz, _ = t.shape\n         device = t.device\n \n         if mode in (\"static\", \"constant_velocity\"):\n@@ -162,10 +174,24 @@ class TargetMotion(nn.Module):\n         body_pos = body[\"positions\"]\n         body_rot = body[\"rotations\"]\n \n-        local_pos, local_rot, corners_local = self.armer_layout(\n-            n_armors, radius, radius_offsets, height_offsets\n+        local_pos, local_rot, corners_local = self.armor_layout(\n+            n_armors,\n+            radius,\n+            radius_offsets,\n+            height_offsets,\n+            params.get(\"armor_pitch\", None),\n         )\n \n         centers = body_pos.unsqueeze(2) + torch.einsum(\n-            \"btij,bnj->btni\", body_rot, local_pos\n+            \"btij,bmj->btmi\", body_rot, local_pos\n+        )\n+        world_rot = torch.einsum(\"btik,bmkj->btmij\", body_rot, local_rot)\n+        corners = centers.unsqueeze(-2) + torch.einsum(\n+            \"btmij,cj->btmci\", world_rot, corners_local\n         )\n+        # facing direction of each plate: local +Z is the plate normal\n+        normal_local = torch.tensor([0.0, 0.0, 1.0], device=device, dtype=radius.dtype)\n+        world_normal = torch.einsum(\"btmij,j->btmi\", world_rot, normal_local)\n+        armor_yaws = torch.atan2(world_normal[..., 1], world_normal[..., 0])\n+\n+        return {\"corners\": corners, \"centers\": centers, \"armor_yaws\": armor_yaws}\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1792654124.75932, "id": "2e1d53a66d76", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_Eq8BLLsGxBvinWdh0Tit8744", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"grep -rn \\\"armor_yaws\\\\|armor_pitch\\\" --include=*.py . | grep -v visualize_target\"}"}], "reasoning": "Original: `local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, zeros)` with yaw = theta. Plate normal = Rz(θ) ez = ez = (0,0,1) constant → all plates horizontal lying flat facing up, but their planes... local XY plane maps to world XY (horizontal). That's the original bug: plates lying flat. Original \"logic\" = one euler call with yaws.\n\nSo the fix should preserve the style: one euler call using yaws and pitch. The physically correct upright armor with a pitch tilt: rotation that maps local X → tangential, local Y → up-ish, local Z → outward normal tilted by pitch. As shown, euler Rz@Ry@Rx can't do exactly that with a single call... unless we rearrange: we need R = Rz(θ) @ (some fixed rotation depending on p). Since Rz@Ry@Rx, and we need R = Rz(θ) @ A(p), A(p) = Ry(a) Rx(r). We want A(p) columns: ex→(0,1,0), ey→(-sin p, 0, cos p), ez→(cos p, 0, sin p). Can this equal Ry(a)Rx(r)? Compute Ry(a)Rx(r):\n\nRy(a) = [[ca,0,sa],[0,1,0],[-sa,0,ca]]\nRx(r) = [[1,0,0],[0,cr,-sr],[0,sr,cr]]\nProduct = [[ca, sa sr, sa cr],[0, cr, -sr],[-sa, ca sr, ca cr]]\n\nColumns: ex→(ca, 0, -sa); ey→(sa sr, cr, ca sr); ez→(sa cr, -sr, ca cr).\n\nWe want ex→(0,1,0): requires ca=0, -sa=0, and second component 0 — impossible. So no.\n\nThus with this euler convention, exact physical upright+radial pitch tilt is impossible in one call unless we interpret differently. But do we need the pitch to tilt the normal? The armor_pitch parameter in the visualization is 15°, and its purpose in the original was... original code ignored armor_pitch entirely (`euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, zeros)`). Wait no—original signature had no armor_pitch; the plate normal was ez always, i.e., plates face up with yaw θ about z: normal constant (0,0,1), plate lying flat, rotated in-plane by θ. So original \"armor_pitch\" wasn't a thing in target.py; it appeared only in the visualization script? Actually the summary says TargetMotion.forward has `params.get(\"armor_pitch\")` — added in session.\n\nWait, actually in the original, normal = (0,0,1) regardless of θ, meaning plates are horizontal disks facing up with yaw spin about z — a \"fan\" pattern. That's clearly the bug: they should face radially outward. To fix with minimal change (same single euler call): `euler_to_rotmat(yaws, pi/2, zeros)`? R = Rz(θ) Ry(90°): normal = Rz Ry(90) ez = Rz(1,0,0)?? Ry(90) ez = (sin90,0,cos90) = (1,0,0) = radial at θ=0? Then Rz(θ)(1,0,0) = (cosθ, sinθ, 0) = radial ✓. And local X → Rz Ry(90) ex = Rz(0,0,-1) = (0,0,-1) → width is vertical! local Y → ey → (0,1,0) tangential → height tangential. Again width/height swapped.\n\nSo with `euler_to_rotmat(yaws, pi/2, zeros)` the plate is upright with normal radial, but width vertical and height tangential (swapped dims). Since armor_width=0.12 (0.12 wide) and height 0.06, swapping gives a tall thin plate 0.06 wide × 0.12 tall. Not right.\n\nAlternatively `euler_to_rotmat(yaws, 0, pi/2)`? R = Rz Rx(90): normal = Rz(0,-1,0) = (sinθ, -cosθ, 0) — radial (negative radial, i.e., inward; fine, direction sign doesn't matter for a plate). local X → ex → Rz(θ) ex = (cosθ, sinθ,0) tangential ✓ width tangential. local Y → Rx(90) ey = (0,0,1) → up ✓ height vertical. \n\nSo `euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, pi/2)` gives exactly upright armor: width tangential, height up, normal inward radial. The normal after Rz(θ)Rx(90): ez→(0,-1,0)→Rz: (sinθ, -cosθ, 0) which is -radial. Fine (outward/inward sign irrelevant visually; but `armor_yaws` computed via atan2 gives θ - 90°? atan2(-cosθ, sinθ) = θ - π/2. That would matter for downsteam armor_yaws semantics!).\n\nHmm. We want normal outward = (cosθ, sinθ, 0) so that armor_yaws == θ. With Rz(θ) Ry(-90)? normal = Rz Ry(-90) ez = Rz(-1,0,0) = (-cosθ,-sinθ,0) = -radial. With Ry(+90) we get +radial (normal = Rz Ry(90) ez = Rz(1,0,0) = (cosθ, sinθ,0)) ✓ but width vertical.\n\nAlternatively yaw offset: `euler_to_rotmat(yaws + pi/2, 0, pi/2)`: R = Rz(θ+90) Rx(90): normal = Rz(θ+90)(0,-1,0) = (cos? ) Rz(φ)(0,-1,0) = (sinφ, -cosφ,0) with φ=θ+90: (cosθ, sinθ, 0) ✓ outward radial, armor_yaws = θ ✓. local X → Rz(θ+90) ex = (cos(θ+90), sin(θ+90),0) = (-sinθ, cosθ, 0) tangential ✓ width tangential. local Y → Rz(θ+90) (0,0,1) = (0,0,1) up ✓.\n\nSo `euler_to_rotmat(yaws + math.pi/2, zeros, math.pi/2)` is the correct upright armor with outward normal! This matches the adopted formula but with pitch zero. Now where does pitch go? We need pitch as a rotation about the tangential axis (local X) after making it upright — i.e., tilt normal up/down. With euler, adding pitch in the middle (Y slot) acts about local Y after Rx, which is world Z-ish? Let's see with pitch in Y slot: R = Rz(θ+90) Ry(p) Rx(90). As computed, Ry rotates about local Y before it's rotated by Rz... but Ry(p) Rx(90) applied to ez = Ry(p)(0,-1,0) = (0,-1,0) → no pitch effect on normal, and it rotates the plate in-plane. Same as before.\n\nTo tilt the plate about the tangential (width) axis, which is the final local X direction, we'd need an Rx after the Rz — i.e. Rz applied before... not possible with Rz@Ry@Rx (leftmost applied last).\n\nAlternatively, express pitch through a different mechanism: since the euler is Rz(yaw) Ry(pitch) Rx(roll) — with roll = π/2 the plate normal after Rx is -y; then Ry(pitch) rotates about y: normal unchanged, but the plate rotates in-plane (the plane xz). Yes, pitch just spins the plate in its own plane when it's vertical. Actually that's kind of what a \"pitch\" tilt would look like? No—tilting about tangential (horizontal axis in the plate) is exactly in-plane rotation for a vertical plate! Wait, in-plane rotation rotates width toward height, but a physical tilt about the tangential axis rotates the plate out of the vertical plane, tilting the normal. Hmm, not the same.\n\nHold on. Maybe the intended interpretation of \"vertical armor\" in this env is simply armor standing vertically, and the \"armor_pitch\" 15° is a small tilt of the plate normal up/down (like RoboMaster armor tilted 15°). The previous session's visualization used the adopted formula which flattens into in-plane rotation, and produced an image that \"looked right\" (35.7×17.1 px). But the physical plate tilt is then wrong (the plate stays perfectly vertical, only its width direction tilts 15°). Whose concern? The user cares about the visualization looking sensible; the summary says extent (0, 0.12, 0.06) at pitch=0 and effect validated.\n\nBut my self-test asserted |nz| = sin(pitch), which is physically what a pitch tilt should do. It fails because the formula does in-plane rotation instead. What is the desired behavior to \"按原来的逻辑写\"? The original logic had no armor_pitch forwarding; plate normal vertical (0,0,1) and yaw θ in-plane. The fix changes it to upright radial. armor_pitch came from the visualization script's --armor-pitch-deg... Actually no: the summary says make_scene passes armor_pitch=math.radians(args.armor_pitch_deg) and the target forwards it. Was --armor-pitch-deg in the original visualization script? The visualization script is new (scripts/ untracked). So armor_pitch integration was my invention this session (to make the plate slightly tilted).\n\nGiven the user's requirement \"不要加复杂的模式定义和字符串判定\" and \"只保留 vertical 作为默认值，按原来的逻辑写\", the cleanest is:\n\n- In target.py: `local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws + math.pi / 2, zeros, torch.full_like(yaws, math.pi / 2))` — vertical upright plates with radial outward normals, exactly the physical \"vertical\" configuration, no pitch involvement? But then armor_pitch param becomes unused -> should remove the whole armor_pitch plumbing (it was added during this session and doesn't improve). Hmm, \"只保留vertical作为默认值\" — vertical means plates upright, normal radial horizontal. Then armor_pitch is meaningless/zero for vertical armor. Simplest: remove armor_pitch entirely from target.py and from the script's CLI? But the summary says the user asked only to keep vertical; armor-pitch-deg default 15 was used to reproduce the old env's tilt? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe armor_pitch in the original env means the plates are tilted relative to the body's vertical axis by 15° (this is the RoboMaster standard: armor plates tilted 15°). Looking at the original code, `euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, zeros)` → normal (0,0,1), i.e., pitch = 90° from vertical? No.\n\nLet's look at how armor_yaws is consumed elsewhere (grep armor_yaws) to understand the expected semantics of armor_pitch."}
