{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1792654184.808251, "id": "ca94010d8d33", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_Dm6CFeJqQvCNyXNhmCVE8608", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/check_geom2.py <<\\\"PYEOF\\\"\\nimport math, sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\".\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport torch\\nfrom diff_env.target import TargetMotion\\nfrom scripts.visualize_target import face_normals\\n\\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\\ntm = TargetMotion()\\nt = torch.zeros(1, 1)\\nout = tm(t, \\\"complex\\\", p0=torch.tensor([[3.0, 0.0, 0.5]]),\\n         yaw_rate=torch.tensor([math.pi / 2]), n_armors=4,\\n         radius=torch.tensor([0.3]),\\n         armor_height_offsets=torch.tensor([[0.05, -0.05, 0.05, -0.05]]))\\nfor k, v in out.items():\\n    print(k, tuple(v.shape))\\nc = out[\\\"corners\\\"][0, 0].detach().numpy()\\ncen = out[\\\"centers\\\"][0, 0].detach().numpy()\\nprint(\\\"corners plate0:\\\\n\\\", np.round(c[0], 4))\\nprint(\\\"centers:\\\\n\\\", np.round(cen, 4))\\nprint(\\\"normals:\\\\n\\\", np.round(face_normals(c), 6))\\nprint(\\\"armor_yaws deg:\\\", np.degrees(out[\\\"armor_yaws\\\"][0, 0].detach().numpy()))\\nPYEOF\\n.venv/bin/python /tmp/check_geom2.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Whoa — the body rotation is applied (\"complex\" mode includes body yaw? and pitch/roll amplitudes), so the world normals are rotated by body pitch (body pitch_0 maybe nonzero → plate normals tilted). nz=0.95?! That's nearly flat. Hmm, wait: extent z 0.13 for a plate 0.12 wide? The z extent 0.13 > height 0.06 — this suggests the plate plane is horizontal-ish, i.e., normals near up. But the body rotation must be weird. Let me check BodyMotion defaults for \"complex\": maybe pitch_0 = π/2? Let me look.\n\nActually `armor_yaws` ~0, 90, -180, -90 despite body rotation? atan2 of normal: normal (-0.3047, 0.0609, 0.9505) → atan2(0.06, -0.30) = 168°, not 0. But printed armor_yaws = 0, 90, 180, -90. Contradiction → my normal computation from corners uses ordering that differs? Wait the printed `nn` rows are all the same-ish: (-0.3047, ±0.061, 0.9505)! All four plates have nearly the same normal?? That can't be right; armor_yaws says 0/90/180/-90.\n\nHmm, the `nn` computation: np.cross(c[:,1]-c[:,0], c[:,3]-c[:,0]) where c has shape (4,4,3). That's the same as face_normals? face_normals uses corners[..., 1, :] etc. Same. So normals should differ per plate.\n\nWait the printed normals all ≈ (-0.3047, ±0.061, 0.9505). That's suspicious — maybe corners have NaNs? No. Let me print corners[0]. Also armor_yaws uses world_normal = world_rot @ [0,0,1], which for plate 0 gave ~0°, i.e., normal ≈ +X mostly, which conflicts with nn row 0 (-0.30, 0.06, 0.95). So corners' implied normal differs from world_rot's normal → the corner loop plane normal from cross(c1-c0, c3-c0) isn't the rotation's +Z?\n\ncorners = centers + world_rot @ corners_local where corners_local are in local XY plane with +Z normal. So cross(c1-c0, c3-c0) = cross(world_rot @ (c1l-c0l), world_rot @ (c3l-c0l)) = world_rot @ cross(...) = world_rot @ (0,0,w*h) → normal. Unless the corners_local ordering is not a proper rectangle order: [-w,-h],[w,-h],[w,h],[-w,h] → c1-c0 = (2w,0,0), c3-c0 = (0,2h,0); cross = (0,0,4wh) ✓.\n\nHmm, but wait: this check ran with armor_height_offsets given, whereas the earlier self-test scene used offsets (0.05,-0.05,...). Fine.\n\nHold on, the printing: `print(np.round(nn, 6))` shows all rows identical-ish. And armor_yaws differs. Something is off: maybe the body motion \"complex\" mode rotates plate 0 by yaw+roll such that corners become weird? But then normals would still differ across plates.\n\nOH WAIT. I see: `nn = np.cross(c[:, 1] - c[:, 0], c[:, 3] - c[:, 0])` where c shape (4,4,3): c[:,1] is (4,3), c[:,0] is (4,3) — fine per plate. Hmm.\n\nUnless... `c` is not (4,4,3)? corners[0] shape is (T?, M, 4, 3)? With t shape (1,1), corners[0] is (M=4, 4, 3)? Wait out[\"corners\"] shape: (bsz, T, M, 4, 3) = (1,1,4,4,3). corners[0] shape (1,4,4,3)! Because t has sln=1. So c[:,1] indexes T dimension... no: c shape (1,4,4,3) → c[:,1] selects time index 1 which doesn't exist... it would fail. Unless out[\"corners\"][0] shape (4,4,3) because t.shape (1,1) gives T=1 and the output is (bsz, T, ...) → (1,1,4,4,3) → [0] = (1,4,4,3). Hmm then c[:,1]-c[:,0] shape (1,4,3): works (indexing T=1? c[:, 1] takes index 1 along dim0 which has size 1 → error). Since no error, out[\"corners\"][0] must be (4,4,3), i.e., output is (bsz, M, 4, 3)?? But then T=1... Let me check: it printed extent shape (1,4,3) meaning c-n[:,None,:] → c (4,4,3) minus (4,1,3)?? That gives (4,4,3). Output shows (1,4,3). So c is (1,4,4,3)? Hmm: n = centers[0] shape? If centers is (bsz, T, M, 3) = (1,1,4,3), n=(1,4,3). Then c-n[:,None,:] with c=(1,4,4,3) - (1,1,4,3) = (1,4,4,3)? But printed extent is (1,4,3). So c = (1,4,3)?? That means corners[0] = (T?, ...)...\n\nLet me just print shapes. Something's off: maybe the \"complex\" mode squeezes time or my t shape (1,1) leads to corners shape (1, 1, 4, 4, 3) and centers (1,1,4,3), and extent computed c - n[:,None,:] = (1,4,4,3) minus (1,1,4,3)... that's (1,4,4,3). Printed two brackets: [[[...]]]? The output shows extent per plate with shape (1,4,3) — three brackets deep? It printed:\n[[[0.5999999 0.3 0.13]\n  [0.3 0.6 0.07] ...]]\nThat's (1,4,3). So c - n[:,None,:] is (1,4,3): means c = (1,4,3)?? and n = (1,4,3), n[:,None,:] = (1,1,3) → broadcast to (1,4,3). So corners[0] is (1,4,3)?! That means corners shape is (bsz, T, M, 4, 3)? No...\n\nHmm, maybe out[\"corners\"] shape is (bsz, M, 4, 3) with t shape (1,1) → returns per-step? Let's just check shapes directly. Also the printed normals then are garbage due to shape confusion.\n\nThe important question: is the world normal correct? Let me redo the check carefully with explicit shapes and using face_normals from the script."}
