usible.\n\nSo the user possibly edited `target.py` (maybe experimenting with the rotation to see the axes) right before asking \"can the script draw the axes of each coordinate system?\" — they may have been trying to understand the orientation, hence the question.\n\nGiven that, I should NOT revert their edit without asking. But the request is to draw axes — I can implement that independent of the rotation formula. Then in my summary, point out that the current `target.py` gives tangential normals (armor_yaws = θ - 90°), which differs from what we validated earlier, and ask whether that was intentional or should be `yaws + half_pi`.\n\nHmm. But wait: maybe the user's edit is actually what they want, and they're asking to draw axes to inspect. Fine.\n\nYet there's a conflict: my earlier self-test/verification claims in the previous answer referenced radial normals. The user may have undone something inadvertently.\n\nLet me look for evidence: does the file have any other changes indicating manual editing? Compare to what I left: my version had:\n```\n        yaws = theta.unsqueeze(0).expand(bsz, -1)\n        # vertical armor: width tangential, height up, normal radial outward\n        zeros = torch.zeros_like(yaws)\n        half_pi = torch.full_like(yaws, math.pi / 2)\n        local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws + half_pi, zeros, half_pi)\n```\nCurrent: identical except `yaws` instead of `yaws + half_pi`. A single-token change — consistent with a manual edit or an accidental undo in an editor.\n\nI'll implement the axes, and mention the discrepancy clearly, offering to restore `+ half_pi` (one-token change) — or perhaps better: restore it? The user's instruction in the previous turn was to keep vertical armor; the tangential orientation isn't vertical armor. But modifying a file the user may have changed manually is risky.\n\nHmm, the system prompt says: \"Mutating work should be intentional and minimal: change what the task needs and nothing else\". The task: draw axes. So minimal = don't touch the rotation. But the result will show plates tangential, and my summary should note it.\n\nWait, but maybe the \"user edit\" is actually from a *previous* state I left: did I ever write `yaws` without +half_pi? Let me search my patch scripts: /tmp/patch_target.py has +half_pi. No other target.py writer except add_rot and patch_vis2.\n\nHmm, one more possibility: the workspace has an auto-formatter/hook that rewrote? No, it would preserve semantics.\n\nWhatever the cause, the state is what it is. Let me check whether there's a git reflog/stash? The file is uncommitted; git diff shows the current state only.\n\nDecision: keep target.py as-is (except the added return keys). Draw axes. In the final message, flag the change: \"注意：当前 target.py 里是 euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, pi/2)，法向是切向的（armor_yaws = θ-90°）；之前验证过的直立径向版本是 yaws + pi/2。如果这是你手动改的，我保持不动；要改回来说一声。\"\n\nHmm, but wait. Before assuming, let me test the current visual: with tangential plates, the camera view shows a fin edge-on? At yaw=0, plate at (3.3,0) has its face normal ±Y; the camera looks along +X, so it sees the plate edge-on (width radial along X → seen nearly as a line? Actually width along X is along the view direction, so projected width ~0; height 0.06 → 16 px vertical). Meanwhile plates A1/A3 at θ=90/270 (centers (3,±0.3)) have normals (sinθ,-cosθ,0)= (1,0,0) and (-1,0,0) → facing along X, i.e., front/back to the camera. So the near-side plates face the camera. The image would show mostly the two side plates facing us. That's also \"plausible-looking\" — maybe that's why the user didn't notice.\n\nHmm, wait. Is it possible that the *intended* design is plates at the sides facing forward/backward? No: for a robot, the front armor plate (at the +X side of the body) faces +X (toward the camera when the camera is at origin and the target is at +X). With `yaws` only, the front-most plate A2 (at body x=2.7, which is the side facing the camera) has normal -Y/tangential? Let me compute: A2 is at θ=180 → normal (sin180, -cos180, 0) = (0, 1, 0) → still tangential. So the plate closest to the camera presents its edge! The plates at the sides (θ=0/180? no) ... anyway the frontal plate faces sideways — clearly wrong.\n\nSo target.py currently has a bug (whatever its origin). Since my task involves showing frames, and the previous verified state used `+ half_pi`, I think the right move is:\n1. Implement axes.\n2. Draw attention to the discrepancy and fix it? The user asked \"能不能把各个坐标系的三维坐标轴画出来\" — a feature request; they didn't ask to change geometry. But if their file was accidentally changed, leaving it means the visualization is wrong.\n\nSince the user's earlier explicit requirement was upright/vertical armor with radial normals (verified), and the current one-token state contradicts it, I suspect an accidental edit. But I shouldn't assume.\n\nHmm, let me check the git status timestamps again: `scripts/visualize_target.py` mtime 16:03:01 (my write) and target.py 16:02:41 (add_rot). If the user had edited target.py between 15:31 and 16:02, its mtime would show the user's edit time, then add_rot would overwrite at 16:02:41 anyway. So no info.\n\nIs there a filesystem backup? Check for `.target.py.swp`, `.#target.py`, or editor backups in /tmp. Let me look.\n\nActually — maybe I should check whether the file was modified by the user through their editor's undo history... impossible.\n\nLet me look at the actual diff of the rotation hunk vs. what I intended: it's a single deletion of \"+ half_pi\". An editor undo of a single edit would do exactly this (my patch added a multi-line block in one change; undoing it would revert the whole block, not one token). A manual edit would target the token. Either way, the user's editor is the likely source.\n\nHmm, hold on. Could there be another explanation: my `patch_target.py` ran twice? No.\n\nOK. Approach: implement axes; verify with the current file; note in the summary that with the present `yaws` (no `+ pi/2`) the plate normals are tangential and the near plate is edge-on, and ask if they want it restored. Actually — better: since the visualization's correctness depends on it, and the user may not realize, I could restore `+ half_pi` and say so. But that contradicts \"change nothing else\".\n\nLet me weigh: If I leave it, the figure shows armor plates facing sideways — the user will likely say \"看起来又怪了\". If I fix it, I might overwrite an intentional change (unlikely—the change is physically wrong and breaks the earlier verified self-test).\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the tangential orientation might be *correct* in this project's convention. Original code: `local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, zeros)` → Rz(θ): plates in the XY plane (horizontal, lying flat), normal +Z, rotating about Z. The \"vertical\" fix I derived is Rz(θ+90) Rx(90) → normal radial. Is there an alternative \"vertical\" fix: Rz(θ) Rx(90) → normal tangential. Which one makes the plate positions consistent? local_positions = (r cosθ, r sinθ, 0), so plate m sits at angle θ. For the plate to face outward, normal = (cosθ, sinθ, 0) → requires Rz(θ+90)Rx(90). For the plate to be \"tangent\" (like a fan blade), normal = (sinθ, -cosθ,0) → Rz(θ)Rx(90). Which is standard? Armor plates on a rotating turret: each plate's face normal points radially outward. Definitely radial.\n\nAlso the summary from the previous session: \"验证：pitch=0 时法向 (1,0,0)、extent (0, 0.12, 0.06)\" → radial. So the intended design is radial.\n\nGiven that, the current file contradicts the agreed design. I'm fairly confident it's an accidental edit (maybe the user was exploring the rotation, or an undo).\n\nHmm, but the user's message is a feature request; maybe they edited the formula *deliberately* while experimenting to answer \"各个坐标系\". If I \"fix\" it, they may be annoyed. The safest path: don't change target.py's formula; mention the discrepancy in the final message and ask. But the deliverable then shows tangential plates, which is confusing.\n\nAlternative: implement the axes, and in the final message say explicitly: \"画出来后发现当前 target.py 的法向是切向的（代码里是 yaws 而不是 yaws + π/2），与之前验证过的直立径向版本不一致；我没有改动它，如果需要恢复告诉我。\" This is honest and minimal.\n\nBut hold on — maybe better to check with the user's likely intent: they asked \"脚本能不能把各个坐标系的三维坐标轴画出来?\" right after I simplified the script. They probably just want axes. The target.py edit might be theirs (experimenting). Leave it.\n\nHmm, but my role is to deliver correct results. A visualization showing tangentially-oriented armor could be seen as a bug in my script... but the axes are correct per the data.\n\nLet me think about the risk balance:\n- Option A: restore `+ half_pi`, mention it. Risk: overwriting user's intentional edit (low probability, since it's physically wrong). Benefit: correct visualization, consistent with prior verified state.\n- Option B: leave, mention. Risk: user sees weird plates, confusion; but respects their edit.\n\nThe user's previous instruction (summary) was \"只保留 vertical 作为唯一逻辑/默认值\" — vertical armor. With `yaws` only, the armor is still vertical (plates stand upright), just facing tangentially. So it's still \"vertical\" geometrically. Hmm! Is it? Plate normal (0,-1,0) horizontal, plate vertical ✓. So it satisfies \"vertical\" but not \"radial normal\".\n\nUgh. Without knowing who changed it, I'll not modify it, but flag it prominently. Actually... let me check the file's diff against the version I left in a different way: maybe the script that simplified the visualization also altered target.py? No.\n\nWait, actually — maybe the state changed because the *user's editor auto-saved an older buffer*. The user may have had target.py open with an older version (before my patch) and saved it, reverting parts. But only one token differs, plus the comment and blank line are intact from my version. So it's a targeted edit.\n\nHmm, or maybe the user ran an \"undo\" exactly once after my patch (which added `+ half_pi` in a single replace)? An undo would revert the whole patch block, not just one token.\n\nSo a human (or an AI?) changed that one token. The user might be testing/experimenting with orientations to understand frames — which motivates the axes request! They may have removed `+ half_pi` to see the difference, then asked for axes to understand better. In that case, leaving it is right, and axes will help them see exactly this.\n\nOK: leave target.py's formula untouched. In the summary, explicitly call out: \"当前 target.py 用的是 euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, π/2)（法向切向，armor_yaws = θ-90°）；之前验证过的直立径向版本要 yaws + π/2。脚本按现有数据画轴，所以你会看到 A0 的面朝 Y 而不是 X。要改回来说一声（一行）。\"\n\nHmm, but hold on: this could be a misdiagnosis. Let me verify the axis drawing is correct with the current data, i.e., the armor triad x-axis follows the plate width. The check confirmed columns match corner axes. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: maybe I should NOT have added `armor_rotations`/`body_rotations` to target.py without asking? It's a minor API addition needed for axes. Alternatively derive from corners to avoid touching target.py. But body_rotations can't be derived. The addition is reasonable.\n\nWait, actually: with `armor_rotations` returned, the axes drawn will correspond exactly to the data used for corners. Good.\n\nNow verify the render: check that the 3 panels have content and triads. Let me do pixel/ASCII checks for the triads: in panel 1, the world triad at origin should have red/green/blue lines near (0,0,0). Check colors present in panel regions.\n\nAlso verify the figure visually via ASCII for each panel.\n\nLet me run an ASCII check on panel 1 and 2 similar to before, using the axis colors only."}
{"type": "tool_call", "timestamp": 1792656344.4525948, "id": "a405a5c42632", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/ascii_frames.py <<\\\"PYEOF\\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\".\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nfrom PIL import Image\\nfrom scripts.visualize_target import AXIS_COLORS\\n\\na = np.asarray(Image.open(\\\"scripts/target_vis.png\\\").convert(\\\"RGB\\\")).astype(int)\\nh, w = a.shape[:2]\\ncols = np.array([[int(c[i:i + 2], 16) for i in (1, 3, 5)] for c in AXIS_COLORS])\\ndist = np.abs(a[:, :, None, :] - cols[None, None]).sum(-1)\\nbest, ok = dist.argmin(-1), dist.min(-1) < 40\\npanels = {\\\"left\\\": (0, w // 3), \\\"mid\\\": (w // 3, 2 * w // 3), \\\"right\\\": (2 * w // 3, w)}\\nchars = \\\"XYZ\\\"\\nfor name, (xa, xb) in panels.items():\\n    sub = np.zeros_like(ok)\\n    sub[:, xa:xb] = ok[:, xa:xb]\\n    ys, xs = np.where(sub)\\n    print(name, \\\"axis-coloured px:\\\", len(xs))\\n    if not len(xs):\\n        continue\\n    x0, x1, y0, y1 = xs.min(), xs.max() + 1, ys.min(), ys.max() + 1\\n    for iy in range(16):\\n        row = \\\"\\\"\\n        for ix in range(56):\\n            ya, yb = y0 + (y1 - y0) * iy // 16, y0 + (y1 - y0) * (iy + 1) // 16\\n            ba, bb = x0 + (x1 - x0) * ix // 56, x0 + (x1 - x0) * (ix + 1) // 56\\n            block = sub[ya:yb, ba:bb]\\n            row += \\\".\\\" if not block.any() else chars[np.bincount(best[ya:yb, ba:bb][block]).argmax()]\\n        print(row)\\n    print()\\nPYEOF\\n.venv/bin/python /tmp/ascii_frames.py\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_1UkpmzIg3uaKq7UMaBL69970", "ui": {"group": null, "chip": "name", "summary": "", "preview": "command", "header": "", "form": "block", "body": "text", "collapse": "never", "highlight": "", "chrome": "", "mutates": true, "undo": false}, "path_arg": ""}
